# The pages and sitemap.xml are generated by build.js and byte-compared by
# `build.js --check`. That comparison only means anything if every platform sees the
# same bytes, so text files are checked out LF everywhere — including on Windows.
#
# `text=auto` on its own was not enough, and the way it fell short is worth keeping.
# It normalises what git STORES (LF, and the stored bytes have always been LF) but
# leaves the CHECKOUT native, so a Windows working tree came out uniform CRLF —
# sources, templates and pages all agreeing, exactly as intended. The gap is that
# build.js does not only copy bytes out of those files. tools/seo.js generates the
# JSON-LD block and the sitemap rows itself, joined with '\n', so the written file was
# CRLF with a few LF islands while the committed file was uniform CRLF. `--check` then
# called three untouched guide pages and sitemap.xml stale on a fresh clone: three
# bytes and eight bytes of difference, every one of them a \n against a \r\n.
#
# Pinning only the generated files would be worse rather than better. build.js splices
# the sources into the pages, so LF-pinned outputs built from CRLF sources would differ
# on every line instead of in three bytes. Whatever the build reads has to be pinned
# too, and that is every text file here, so the rule stays blanket.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Vendored third-party builds are the exception: they are byte-for-byte upstream
# artefacts, and vendor/README.md records their checksums so an update can be shown
# to be the real file. Normalisation rewrites those bytes and makes the checksums
# wrong, which is exactly what happened when they were first committed.
vendor/*.js -text
